1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Techotlalatzin from Codex Xolotl
2 CONT
2 CONT Techotlalatzin (or Techotlala, removing the Classical Nahuatl honorific -tzin) was the ruler (tlatoani) of the pre-Columbian Mesoamerican city-state of Texcoco from 1357[1] or 1377[2] until his death in 1409. Techotlalatzin was th
2 CONC e first ruler of the Acolhua who actively adopted the prevailing culture of the Valley of Mexico, including the Nahuatl language.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Quinatzin II, Techotlalatzin was able to build a small Acolhua-dominated domain on the eastern side of Lake Texcoco, although this domain was apparently under the influence or even loose control of the Tepanec empire of
2 CONC  his contemporary, Tezozomoc of Azcapotzalco.
2 CONT
2 CONT Techotlalatzin was succeeded by his son, Ixtlilxochitl I, who challenged the power of Tezozomoc and lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early sources for Techotlalatzin include Fray Juan de Torquemada, Fernando de Alva Cortés Ixtlilxochitl, Juan Bautista de Pomar, and Codex Xolotl, although these sources at times present conflicting information (in fact, they are 
2 CONC often internally inconsistent). Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     Offner (1979, p.231).
2 CONT     Davies (1980, p.61).
2 CONT     Davies (1980, p.129); Smith (1984, p.170). Smith himself further references Fernando de Alva Cortés Ixtlilxochitl, Juan Bautista de Pomar, and Chimalpahin.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     Davies, Nigel (1980). The Toltec Heritage: From the Fall of Tula to the Rise of Tenochtitlan (Civilization of the American Indian Series, Vol. 153). Norman: University of Oklahoma Press. ISBN 0-8061-1505-X.
2 CONT     Offner, Jerome A. (1979). "A Reassessment of the Extent and Structuring of the Empire of Techotlalatzin, Fourteenth Century Ruler of Texcoco". Ethnohistory (Duke University Press) 26 (3): pp.231–241. doi:10.2307/481560. JSTOR 
2 CONC 481560.
2 CONT     Smith, Michael E. (1984). "The Aztlan Migrations of Nahuatl Chronicles: Myth or History?" (PDF). Ethnohistory (Duke University Press) 31 (3): pp.153–186. doi:10.2307/482619. JSTOR 482619.
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT
2 CONT     Wikisource-logo.svg "Techotlalatzin". Appletons' Cyclopædia of American Biography. 1889.
2 CONT
