1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Note: Some sources say that Chimalpopoca was the son of Huitzilihuitl, the previous ruler, while others say that he was a son of Acamapichtli, the first ruler of Tenochtitlan, making him Huitzilihuitl's brother. Ge
2 CONC rónimo de Mendieta, in his Historia eclesiástica indiana, notes the discrepancy and concludes that Huitzilihuitl, Chimalpopoca and Itzcoatl (Chimalpopoca's successor) must have been brothers, based on his understanding on the Azte
2 CONC c system of succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chimalpopoca''' (Classical Nahuatl: Chīmalpopōca [tʃiːmaɬpoˈpoːka] for "smoking shield") (1397–1427) was the third Tlatoani of Tenochtitlan or Aztec Emperor (1417–1427).
2 CONT
2 CONT == Rule ==
2 CONT
2 CONT On the day of Chimalpopoca's coronation in 1417 (some sources say 1416 or 1418), his Tlacaelel was named high priest. From this point on the ecclesiastical and governmental offices among the Aztecs were separate.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he assumed the throne at age 20, Tenochtitlan was a tributary of the Tepanec city of Azcapotzalco, which was ruled by his Tezozomoc. This alliance, and the Mexicas' position within it, was strengthened by Tenochtitlan's loyal
2 CONC ty during Tezozomoc's 1418 war with Ixtlilxochitl I of Texcoco. The conquered city was granted to Tenochtitlan as a tributary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nezahualcoyotl, displaced prince of Texcoco, was living in the mountains. Chimalpopoca interceded with Tezozomoc on his behalf, and Tezozomoc agreed to allow Netzahualcoyotl to live in Tenochtitlan under his protection.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1426 Tezozómoc assisted Chimalpopoca in the construction of a new aqueduct. This aqueduct was of wood, and ran from the elevated place of Chapultepec to Tenochtitlan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chimalpopoca also had a causeway constructed to Tlacopan. The causeway contained openings spanned by wooden bridges, which were removed at night. Also during his reign he dedicated a stone for sacrifices in the Tlacocomoco section
2 CONC  of Tenochtitlan. To him is attributed the conquest of Tequizquiac.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death ===
2 CONT
2 CONT When Tezozomoc died in 1427 after a long reign, he was succeeded by his son Tayatzin. However Maxtla, ruler of Coyoacán and older half-brother of Tayatzin, soon incited a rebellion among the nobles of Azcapotzalco and usurped the 
2 CONC throne. Chimalpopoca allied with Tayatzin, and the two conspired to retake the throne and kill Maxtla. Friendly relations between Tenochtitlan and Azcapotzalco were thus replaced by insults and violent intrigue, apparently involvi
2 CONC ng tit-for-tat assassination attempts. Maxtla sent Chimalpopoca a present of women's clothing, and insulted his lover.
2 CONT
2 CONT Teyatzin was killed, and Chimalpopoca decided to offer himself as a sacrifice at the altar of his father Huitzilíhuitl.[verification needed] Some of his nobles joined him. In the middle of the ceremony, before the tlatoani's turn,
2 CONC  an expeditionary force from Azcapotzalco invaded Tenochtitlan, took Chimalpopoca prisoner and carried him to Azcapotzalco. There he was exhibited in a cage and kept on starvation rations. He committed suicide, hanging himself fro
2 CONC m a beam with his belt. (Another account says he was strangled by his captors.)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded as tlatoani by Itzcoatl. http://www.geni.com/people/Izcoatl-4th-Aztec-Emperor/6000000007453720795
2 CONT
2 CONT === Notes ===
2 CONT
2 CONT # Durán's Historia de las Indias de Nueva España, Chimalpahin's third and seventh Relaciones, the Crónica Mexicayotl, the Leyenda de los Soles, Ixtlilxochitl's Relaciones and his Historia Chichimeca, the Codex Xolotl and the Cróni
2 CONC ca Mexicana.
2 CONT # Mendieta's Historia eclesiástica indiana, Motolinia's Memoriales, the Origen de los Mexicanos, the Relación de la Genealogía, and the Historia de los Mexicanos por sus pinturas.
2 CONT # Gillespie (1989: pp. 12–14.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Links ===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Wikipedia''' http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimalpopoca
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Some sources say that Chimalpopoca was the son of Huitzilihuitl, the previous ruler, while others say that he was a son of Acamapichtli, the first ruler of Tenochtitlan, making him Huitzilihuitl's brother. Gerónimo de Mendieta, in
2 CONC  his Historia eclesiástica indiana, notes the discrepancy and concludes that Huitzilihuitl, Chimalpopoca and Itzcoatl (Chimalpopoca's successor) must have been brothers, based on his understanding on the Aztec system of succession
2 CONC .
