1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Summary of Isabel de Moctezuma:
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationships
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT *Father: Motecuhzoma (or Moctezuma II)
2 CONT *Mother: Tecalco (or Teotlalco, or Tayhualcan)
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT *2. Axayacatl (murdered by Cuauhtemoc, likely to prevent division among the Mexica leadership, some of whom wanted to continue to cede concessions to the Spanish).
2 CONT
2 CONT Numerous half-siblings (considering there were anything from 600-3000 concubines of her father, sources vary on the number, their actual number is probably too numerous to exhaustively count or list).
2 CONT
2 CONT Husbands and partners:
2 CONT *First Husband: Atlixcatzin (according to Juan Ecuatlatoa, witness in her 1548 suit), before 1519
2 CONT *Second Husband: Cuitlahuac, 10th tlahtoani of México-Tenochtitlan, after July 1, 1520.
2 CONT *Third Husband: Cuauhtemoc, 11th tlahtoani of México-Tenochtitlan, after Sept. 18, 1520
2 CONT *Fourth Husband: Alonso de Grado, visitador general de indios, June 1526 (he dies 1527 "a natural death")
2 CONT *Probable partner: Hernán Cortés, Conquistador (impregnated during her second long-term stay at his home in Coyoacan, present southern Mexico City, following Alonso de Grado's death)
2 CONT *Fifth Husband: Pedro Gallego de Andrade (she gave birth about 5 or 6 months after the wedding), circa 1528. (He died April 15, 1531)
2 CONT *Sixth Husband: Juan Cano de Saavedra (later in 1531)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly with Hernán Cortés (only one, out of wedlock):
2 CONT *1. Leonor Cortés Moctezuma (m. Juan de Tolosa), b. c1528, may have been baptized under the name "de Andrade Moctezuma" (Lived separate from her mother, transferred to the home of Juan Altamirano after birth.)
2 CONT
2 CONT With Pedro Gallego de Andrade
2 CONT *1. Juan de Andrade Moctezuma, b. 1529, baptized by Bishop Juan de Zumárraga.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Juan Cano de Saavedra
2 CONT *1. Pedro Cano de Moctezuma, 
2 CONT *2. Gonzalo Cano de Moctezuma, 
2 CONT *3. Juan Cano de Moctezuma, 
2 CONT *4. Isabel de Moctezuma (the Minor)
2 CONT *5. Catalina de Moctezuma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information:
2 CONT *Birth: Either during the reign of Ahuitzotl (according to Miguel Tulnahuacatl in court testimony) before 1502, or July 1510 (according to 19th century Mexican historian Alfredo Chavero, who assigned the date probably based on tes
2 CONC timony by Juan Cano de Saavedra, who was reportedly eager to explain why Isabel never formally succeeded Moctezuma, if she was in fact his rightful heir).
2 CONT *Baptism: Likely November 1519, as she and her half-sisters Maria and Leonor, who were given as ransom to Cortes, were recorded by their baptismal names in contemporaneous records. But could be any time between November 1519 and J
2 CONC une 30, 1520, during captivity under Cortes, or after Aug. 13, 1521, while in Cortes' custody again.
2 CONT *Marriages and liaisons: Listed with husbands and partners.
2 CONT *Death: July 11, 1550, according to researcher Amada López de Meneses
2 CONT *Burial: Unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timeline:
2 CONT *c1466" Birth of father Moctezuma II
2 CONT *1486-1502: Reign of Ahuilzotl
2 CONT *1519 November: Given with Maria and Leonor (baptismal names) as hostages. Reportedly adolescent "maidens" at the time of being taken hostage, though Juan Ecuatlatoa testified in 1548 that she was in fact married).
2 CONT *1520 June 30: Moctezuma, her father, dies after being pelted by projectiles while trying to calm his people following Cortes' return to Tenochitlan. Legend says that at deathbed, Moctezuma asks Cortes to be sure that his "favorit
2 CONC e" daughter ("main and legitimate heir" according to Cortes in 1526) is well-cared for.
2 CONT *1520 July 1: After the Noche Triste, when much of Cortes' men (and many of Moctezuma's children, taken as hostages during the flight) are killed trying to escape Tenochitlan, Isabel, or Tecuichpo, is freed of Spanish captivity, a
2 CONC nd married shortly after to Cuitlahuac, who claims succession of Mexica. He rules for 80 days.
2 CONT *1520 Sept 18: Likely death date of Cuitlahuac, 80 days after the death of Moctezuma. Cuauhtemoc, Tecuichpo's uncle, establishes his succession after killing Isabel's brother, Axayacatl, who was apparently attempting to make peace
2 CONC  with the Spanish near Tlaxcala (possibly as a collaboration to gain supremacy in what would have been a civil war in Mexica). 
2 CONT *1521 Aug. 13: Captured with her husband Cuauhtemoc on one of the causeways out of Tenochitlan by men from a Spanish brigantine patrolling Lake Texcoco. Held captive in Cortes' new home in Coyoacan, now in southern Mexico City, se
2 CONC parate from her ill-fated husband.
2 CONT *1522: Catalina Juárez Marcaida, first wife of Hernan Cortes, dies in Coyoacan.
2 CONT *1524, June: In search of the supposed treasure of Moctezuma, Cortes takes Cuauhtemoc from Coyoacan. 
2 CONT *1525, Feb. 28: In present Honduras, Cuauhtemoc is summarily tried for attempting to foment a rebellion against Cortes and hung in the forests near Hubieras. (It is possible that La Malinche, or Marina, Cortes' translator and mist
2 CONC ress, died in the journey south. In any case, her whereabouts are no longer traced after Cortes' return to Coyoacan.)
2 CONT *1526, June: After returning to Coyoacan, Cortes marries Isabel to Alonso de Grado, his appointive visitador general de indios. She receives a dowry of "1,220 houses” in Tlacopan and its dependent towns, villages and communities (
2 CONC in the end, this is reduced to 120 houses, though she always considered the entire town to be hers).
2 CONT *1526, July 5: Cortes is removed from office as ruler of New Spain by royal decree presented by Luis Ponce de Leon, who takes office from him in Ciudad de Mexico and begins his investigation.
2 CONT *1526, July 16: Ponce de Leon becomes deathly ill, and hands over governorship and the case against Cortes to Marcos de Aguilar (who was sent to investigate violations of religious doctrine by Cortes). Ponce de Leon dies 4 days la
2 CONC ter. Aguilar delegates duties to several other men, but retains Cortes as Captain-General (a move that by December he regrets).
2 CONT *1527, March 1: Marco de Aguilar dies in office. His successor, Alonso de Estrada, charges Cortes with poisoning his two predecessors.
2 CONT *1527: Alonso de Grado dies "a natural death" and Isabel returns to Cortes' custody at Coyoacan. During her time there, she becomes impregnated, many (including the daughter-to-come) suspect by Cortes himself.
2 CONT *1528: Terrified of further scandal while being politically persecuted by Estrada, Cortes has Isabel married off immediately to Pedro Gallegos de Andrade.
2 CONT *1528, March: After Estrada orders Cortes exiled from his home in Coyoacan and New Spain in general, Cortes sails with his son Martin back to Spain. 
2 CONT *1528, between June and November: About 5-6 months after Cortes marries her to Pedro Gallegos de Andrade, Isabel gives birth to Leonor. She immediately has the child taken away to Juan Altamirano, Cortes close confidante.
2 CONT *1529, late: Gives birth to Juan de Andrade Moctezuma. The boy is baptized by Bishop Juan de Zumárraga, the highest member of the Catholic Church in Mexico. 
2 CONT *1530: Estrada deprives Isabel of her former dowry of Tlacopan as part of the continuing prosecution against Cortes. Gallego uses all the family fortune in countersuits against Estrada.
2 CONT *1530, late: Cortes returns to Ciudad de Mexico and restores Tlacopan to Isabel, but does little further to assist her as he is in difficult straights himself (even with new titles and honors). Estrada orchestrates a final coup ag
2 CONC ainst him, an attempted prosecution for allegedly murdering Catalina Juárez Marcaida, his first wife, in 1522. The prosecution fails, but prevents Cortes from returning to power.
2 CONT *1531, April 15: Pedro de Gallego de Andrade dies, leaving Isabel and toddler Juan behind. Desperate to survive, Isabel marries shortly after Juan Cano de Saavedra, an ambitious rival of Cortes. He immediately begins a campaign to
2 CONC  restore Isabel to power as the "rightful heir of Moctezuma".
2 CONT *1532, April: Juan Cano commissions an anonymous Franciscan monk to produce a genealogy of Mexica lords showing Isabel's ancestry and a list of their private land holdings. These are presented in the Relation Concerning the Geneal
2 CONC ogy and Lineage of the Lords, who have governed this Land of New Spain, and the Origin of the Mexicans. 
2 CONT *1535, April 17: Antonio de Mendoza, the first Viceroy of New Spain, arrives in Ciudad de Mexico. Cortes retires to Cuernavaca, where he prepares for his final exploration of the Pacific coast of Mexico, which he sets out for the 
2 CONC following year. Cortes is effectively out of her life by this point. (He would return to Spain in 1541, never to return to the New World.)
2 CONT *1542: Juan Cano sets sail for Spain to attempt to assert Isabel's rights as heiress of Moctezuma.
2 CONT *1542, September: Juan Cano spreads word of the inheritance rights he will be seeking in Madrid while visiting friends in Santo Domingo.
2 CONT *1544: Juan Cano returns to New Spain, but without achieving the recognition that he was seeking on behalf of Isabel.
2 CONT *1546: Juan Cano sends a petition to Crown Prince Felipe II to restore lands and towns taken from Isabel. The prince orders a judicial investigation.
2 CONT *1548, November: Juan Cano presents to the Audiencia Real in Ciudad Mexico a new petition listing towns and lands held by Isabel's parents.
2 CONT *1549, January 4: Juan Cano presents a second petition with a detailed list of 117 of those lands, settlements, and buildings belonging to Moctezuma and Isabel's mother, listed as principal wife (the list of her holdings had incre
2 CONC ased from 10 to 39 over the original petition that Juan Cano took to Spain in 1532).
2 CONT *1550, July 11: Isabel dies. Her suit is continued on behalf of her heirs by Juan Cano.
2 CONT *1556, October: The Audiencia Real confirms Isabel's right of inheritance, but observes that since the lands are already held in possession of others, and since their restoration would cause great discontent, it regretfully noted 
2 CONC that enforcement of this decision would be impossible.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Las hijas de Moctezuma, que el Emperador de los Mexica dejó encomendadas a Hernán Cortés, estando en trance de muerte, eran tres. Al menos así lo expresa Cortés en el acta de donación: La mayor su legítima heredera Doña Isabel. A 
2 CONC Isabel le asignó, con el título de "Señora de Tacuba", el pueblo de Tacuba, con sus habitantes, así como Yetepec, Chimalpan, Jilocingo y Ecatepec, más otras estancias, sumando en total mil doscientas casas (cfr. J. Miralles, Herná
2 CONC n Cortés, inventor de México, Tusquets, México 2002, p. 434). El documento está firmado el 27 de junio de 1526. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT "Doña Isabel de Moctezuma: the emperor’s favourite daughter?" by Anastasia Kalyuta, ethnohistorian with the Russian Museum of Ethnography, St. Petersburg
2 CONT
