2 CONT *1994 Códice Cozcatzin. 2 vols, Mexico: INAH.
2 CONT *1992 Códice Techialoyan García Granados, Toluca: Gobierno del Estado de México, Secretaría de Finanzas y Planeación.
2 CONT *Corominas y Pascual Juan, 1954 Diccionario Crítico Etimológico de la Lengua Castellana 4 vols, Bern: Editorial Franke.
2 CONT *Díaz del Castillo Bernal, 1975 Historia Verdadera de la Conquista de la Nueva España. Barcelona: Editorial Ramón Sopena. 
2 CONT *Gibson, Charles, 1964 The Aztecs under Spanish Rule. Stanford: Stanford University Press.
2 CONT *Icazbalceta García Joaquín ed. 1941 Relación de la Genealogía y Linaje de los Señores que han señoreado Esta Tierra de Nueva España in Nueva Colección de Documentos para la Historia de México. Mexico: Chávez Hayhoe. Pp 263-281
2 CONT *— Orígen de los Mexicanos, México: Chávez Hayhoe. Pp.281-308.
2 CONT *Ixtlilxochitl, Fernando de Alva, 1848 Historia Chichimeca in Antiquities of Mexico, ed. Kingsborough E.K. Vol. 9.205-316. London.
2 CONT *López de Meneses, Amada, 1948 Tecuichpochtzin, Hija de Moteczuma (1510?-1550) Revista de Indias (9):31-47.
2 CONT *Martínez, José L., ed. 1990-91 Documentos Cortesianos: Primera y Segunda Seccion. 3 vols Mexico: UNAM, Fondo de Cultura Económica.
2 CONT *Oviedo y Valdés, Gonzalo Fernández de, 1995, Diálogo del Alcaide de la Fortaleza de la Cuidad de Santo Domingo de la Isla Española Autor y Cronista de Estas Historias, de Una Parte e de la Otra, Un Caballero Vecino de la Gran Cui
2 CONC dad de México llamado Juan Cano in Sucesos y Diálogos de la Nueva España: prológo y selección Edmundo O’Gorman Mexico: UNAM P.117-128. 
2 CONT *Pérez Rocha, Emma, Tena Rafael ed. 2000 La Nobleza Indígena del Centro de México Después de la Conquista. México: INAH.
2 CONT *Sahagún, Bernardino, 1950-82 Florentine Codex: General History of the Things of New Spain. Santa Fe: University Press of Utah.
2 CONT *Zavala, Silvio y colaboradores ed. 1939-42 Epistolario de la Nueva España 1505-1818. Recopilación por Paso y Troncoso Francisco, 16 vols México: Antigua Libreria Robredo de José Porrúa e Hijos.
2 CONT *Zorita, Alonso, 1891 Breve y Sumaria Relación sobre Señores de Nueva Espana in Nueva Colección de Documentos para la Historia de México publicados, por J. García Icazbalceta, México: Vol.3. Pp. 69-205.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabel_Moctezuma
2 CONT
2 CONT Doña Isabel Moctezuma (b. 1509 or 1510 – d. 1550 or 1551) (born Techichipotzin) was a daughter of the Aztec ruler Moctezuma II. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the consort of the Aztec emperors Cuitláhuac and Cuauhtemoc and as such the last Aztec empress. After the Spanish conquest, Doña Isabel was recognized as Moctezuma's legitimate heir, and became one of the Mexican Indians g
2 CONC ranted an encomienda. Among the others were her half-sister Leonor (or Mariana) Moctezuma, and Juan Sánchez, an Indian governor in Oaxaca.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and early marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Doña Isabel's mother was Princess Teotlalco and her birth name was Tecuich(po)tzin, translated as "lord's daughter" in Nahuatl. Teotlalco was Moctezuma's principal wife and, thus, among Moctezuma's daughters Tecuichpotzin had prim
2 CONC acy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a small child, Tecuichpotzin was married to Atlixcatzin, who died by 1520. After her father was killed, either by his own people or the Spanish, she was quickly married to her uncle Cuitláhuac who became emperor after Moctezuma
2 CONC 's death. Cuitlahuac died of smallpox after only sixty days of rule and Cuauhtemoc became emperor and married Tecuichpotzin. She was only about eleven or twelve years old at the time of her third marriage.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Doña Isabel and the Conquest of Tenochtitlan
2 CONT
2 CONT Hernan Cortés and his Spanish army entered Tenochitlan on November 8, 1519, and quickly took Moctezuma as a hostage. He was murdered by the Spanish.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, when the Aztecs revolted and expelled Cortés and his army from Tenochitlan (La Noche Triste, June 30, 1520), Tecuichpotzin was left behind in the city by the Spanish. Aztec leaders quickly married her to Cuitlahuac, the n
2 CONC ew emperor, and, after he died, to Cuauhtemoc. Cortés returned in 1521 with a large army of Spaniards and Indian allies, mostly from Tlaxcala, to assault Tenochitlan. The Aztecs, their numbers and morale depleted by a smallpox epi
2 CONC demic, were defeated. Cuahtemoc and his court attempted to flee Tenochitlan by boat, but they were captured by the Spanish. On surrendering, Cuahtemoc asked the Spanish to respect the ladies of his court, including his young wife 
2 CONC Tecuichpotzin.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1525, Cortés executed Cuauhtemoc and Tecuichpotzin was widowed for the third time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conversion to Christianity and marriage to a Spaniard
2 CONT
2 CONT Cortés valued Tecuichpotzin as a symbol of what he wished to portray as the continuity of rule between the Aztecs and the Spanish. She was instructed in Christianity, converted to Catholicism, probably in 1526, and baptized as Isa
2 CONC bel, the name by which she would thereafter be known. Every indication is that Doña Isabel, the former Aztec princess Tecuichpotzin, was devout in her new religion. She gave generously in alms to the Augustinians, to the point tha
2 CONC t she was asked to stop.[6] Isabel’s education as a Christian did not include teaching her to read and she remained illiterate.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cortés arranged the marriage of Doña Isabel to his close colleague Alonso de Grado in June 1526. Part of the marriage arrangement was the granting of a large encomienda to Doña Isabel. The encomienda consisted of the city of Tacub
2 CONC a (about five miles west of Tenochitlan (now called Mexico City) and was the largest encomienda in the Valley of Mexico, an indicator of the importance Cortés gave to Isabel.[8] The encomienda of Doña Isabel endured for centuries.
2 CONC  The Spanish and, later, Mexican governments, paid royalties in the form of a pension to the descendants of Doña Isabel until 1933 and a Count of Miravalle, the descendants of Moctezuma, still exists in Spain.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cortés, an unwanted child, and two more marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Doña Isabel was described as “very beautiful” and “a very pretty woman for an Indian.”[10] Her fourth husband, Alonso de Grado, died shortly and Isabel, about 17 years old, was widowed for a fourth time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cortes took her into his household and shortly she became pregnant. He quickly married her to another associate, Pedro Gallego de Andrade, and the child, christened Leonor Cortes Moctezuma, was born a few months later. She apparen
2 CONC tly refused to recognize the child who was placed in the care of Juan Gutierrez de Altamirano, another close associate of Cortés. Cortés however accepted the child as his own and ensured that she was brought up well and received a
2 CONC n inheritance from his and Doña Isabel’s estate.[11] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel’s marriage to Gallego produced a son, Juan de Andrade Gallego Moctezuma, born in 1530. However, Gallego died shortly thereafter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1532 she married her sixth husband, Juan Cano de Saavedra, by whom she had three sons and two daughters: Pedro, Gonzalo, Juan, Isabel, and Catalina Cano de Moctezuma. Isabel and Catalina became nuns at the first convent in the 
2 CONC Americas, El Convento de la Conception de la Madre de Dios. Both daughters were well-educated, as presumably were her sons.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT Doña Isabel died in 1550 or 1551 (July 11, 1550, according to researcher Amada López de Meneses). Her estate was large, consisting not only of the encomienda, but also personal possessions she had acquired during her marriage with
2 CONC  the Spaniards. Previous to those marriages she had been an Aztec princess who owned nothing except her distinguished name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her will is one of the few existing indicators of her personality. She directed that her Indian slaves be set free, ordered that one-fifth of the estate be given to the Catholic Church, and that all her outstanding debts, includin
2 CONC g wages owed to servants, be paid. She had acquired jewelry and other luxury items and requested that many of these be given to her daughters and that other property be sold and one-third of the proceeds go to her daughters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a deathbed wish, 20 percent of her estate was to be given to Leonor, her out of wedlock child by Cortés. This was apparently a dowry as Leonor was married, or soon to be married, to Juan de Tolosa in Zacatecas.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel willed the majority of her encomienda to her eldest son, Juan de Andrade, but his inheritance of her encomienda was disputed by her widower, Juan Cano, and Diego Arias de Sotelo, son-in-law of Leonor (Mariana) Moctezuma, wh
2 CONC om he claimed was Moctezuma's true heir. The result after years of litigation was that Arias de Sotelo's claim was dismissed, and Tacuba was divided between Cano and Andrade.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Miravalle line of Spanish royalty began with Isabel's son, Juan de Andrade. Her sons, Pedro and Gonzalo Cano, became prominent citizens of Mexico City. Her son, Juan Cano Moctezuma, married into a prominent family in Cáceres, 
2 CONC Spain where the Palacio de Toledo-Moctezuma still exists.[15] Isabel's last husband, Juan Cano, died in Seville in 1572. [edit]Importance
2 CONT
2 CONT We know very little about Doña Isabel beyond a few facts of her life. She seems to have been more than a mere pawn in the hands of Aztec royalty and Spanish conquerors. Her will reveals her to have been a decisive and strong woman
2 CONC  who was generous and thoughtful. She seems to have made the transition from Aztec princess to Spanish Doña successfully. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her descendants were the most prominent example of her day of mestizaje – the mixture of Spanish and Indian ancestries – that would characterize the future of Mexico. The Spanish wished to inculcate in the Indians "the economic, r
2 CONC eligious, and cultural orientation of Spain."[16] Isabel, whether by desire or necessity, was the first great success of the assimilation of Spanish and Indian.
2 CONT
