1 NOTE {geni:about_me} She was killed in the 1696 San Ildefonso Revolt along with 2 of her children, her mother and brother, and several others.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From notes by GTAChick:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.oocities.org/gtachick/d0/I0000996.HTM
2 CONT
2 CONT 376 iii. Pedro Sánchez De Iñigo [1874] 
2 CONT *was born in Nuevo Mexico 1674.[1875] 
2 CONT *Pedro died before 1720.[1876] 
2 CONT *He married twice. 
2 CONT **He married Leonor Baca in El Real De San Lorenzo, 1691.[1877] (Additional notes for Leonor Baca [1878]) 
2 CONT **He married María Luján in Bernalillo, Kingdom Of New Mexico, before 1700.[1879] (Additional notes for María Luján [1880]) María became the mother of Pedro II Sánchez De Iñigo 1700.[1881] María became the mother of Manuela Sánche
2 CONC z De Iñigo January 13, 1700/01. María became the mother of Francisca Xaviera Sánchez De Iñigo December 13, 1715.[1882]
2 CONT
2 CONT At 26 years of age Pedro became the father of Pedro II Sánchez De Iñigo 1700.[1883] At 26 years of age Pedro became the father of Manuela Sánchez De Iñigo January 13, 1700/01. At 41 years of age Pedro became the father of Francisc
2 CONC a Xaviera Sánchez De Iñigo December 13, 1715.[1884] Pedro and Jacinto Sánchez de Iñigo were two natives of New Mexico, evidently brothers, who escaped the 1680 Indian Massacre as minors, and returned to re-settle New Mexico in 169
2 CONC 3. Or else they were born at Guadalupe del Paso, considered then a part of New Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Sánchez de Iñigo was born "in New Mexico, " the natural son of Ana (Juana) López. On January 7, 1692, he married Leonor Baca at El Real de San Lorenzo [AASF, DM, 1691, No. 3]. A Francisca Sánchez de Iñigo, the wife of Captai
2 CONC n Juan García de Noriega, was most likely his sister. And he could well be a "Pedro López de Yñiguez" who was soldering at Guadalupe del Paso prior to the Reconquest. [AASF, DM, 1694, No. 3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro first settled in the Rio Arriba area, and in 1696 his wife was killed by the Indians of San Ildefonso with her mother, a brother, and her two children. [Old Santa Fe, Vol. III, pp. 332-373].
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnotes:
2 CONT *Apprently not preserved in archive copy (2/23/2013).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the biography of Cristobal de Baca the Younger, section on his children:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogytrails.com/newmex/santafe/bios1.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Ignacio de Baca was born about 1657 in New Mexico.  About 1677 he married Juana de Anaya Almazán. Ignacio was 24 years old when he signed up in 1681 as a captain in the military, married, four small children, and twenty servants. 
2 CONC  He was tall and slim and had an aquiline face, fair complexion, wavy red [probably reddish] hair and no beard.  By 1684 he was a sargento mayor at the presidio of Guadalupe del Paso [El Paso].  As the assistant alcalde of the Rea
2 CONC l de San Lorenzo, he arrested Silvestre Pacheco for killing his brother, José de Baca, who was Pacheco’s brother-in-law. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ignacio’s family was ill-fated.  He died about March 1692 in El Paso, not living to return to New Mexico. He was thirty-four years old. His wife and seven children were in the re-entry.  After a stay in Santa Fe, they settled at S
2 CONC an Ildefonso. When the Pueblos revolted again in 1696, his wife Juana was killed along with two priests of the San Ildefonso Mission.  Ignacio’s son, Alonso, was killed with his mother, and his other son, Andrés, was killed at Nam
2 CONC bé in the same revolt, thus ending the male line.  Two daughters were also killed: '''Leonor de Baca, married to Pedro Sánchez, was killed along with a daughter and son of her own'''; and Rosa de Baca, not yet married, was also ki
2 CONC lled.  Ignacio’s daughters Gerónima, María Magdalena, and Margarita de Baca survived the rebellion. Margarita later married Diego Lucero de Godoy at San Ildefonso in 1716. María Magdalena married Felipe Tamaris.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ben M. Angel notes: The two priests, Francisco Corvera and Antonio Moreno, were barred into their sleeping quarters, which were then set afire. They died of smoke inhalation. History doesn't seem to record how any of the other Sp
2 CONC aniards died in the June 4 rebellion.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT To the Royal Crown Restored: The Journals of Don Diego De Vargas, New Mexico 1692-1694, by  Diego De Vargas, John L. Kessell, Dr Rick Hendricks, Meredith D. Dodge
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.by/books?id=ELhmJlIvvZkC&pg=PA83&lpg=PA83&dq=Leonor+Baca+1696+San+Ildefonso&source=bl&ots=967O_WMSSd&sig=leEFTVz_A3cPv3BCdIrzUXno1x4&hl=ru&sa=X&ei=Wt0oUf31A8XOsgb00oHACQ&ved=0CFwQ6AEwBA#v=onepage&q=Leonor%20Bac
2 CONC a%201696%20San%20Ildefonso&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT 42. Pedro Sánchez de Iñigo, born around 1674, was the natural son of Juana Lopez. In 1692, he married Leonor Baca at the Real of San Lorenzo. Sánchez settled in the Rio Arriba area after the reconquest. In 1696, Indians killed his
2 CONC  wife and all but one of his children at San Ildefonso. Two years later in Santa Cruz, Pedro, a soldier, married Maria Lujan, legitimate daughter of Matias Lujan and Francisca Romero. He was mont those reported dead or missing in 
2 CONC 1695, but was alive as late as 1714 and only died in 1720. NMF, 279-80, CRG, 239. AASF, DM, 1691: 3A; 1698:23; 1714:3.
