1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Golden Fleece - Knights: Spanish Branch====
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_de_Carvajal_y_Lanc%C3%A1ster '''José de Carvajal y Lancaster.''']
2 CONT * José de Carvajal y Lancáster (1698 – 1754) was a Spanish statesman.
2 CONT * He was son of the duke of Linares and his mother was descendant of Jorge de Lancastre, a natural son of King John II of Portugal. After graduating at the University of Salamanca, he was appointed oidor (judge) of the Chancillerí
2 CONC a of Valladolid and later councillor of the Council of the Indies. Prime minister José del Campillo elected him as his personal secretary. In 1746, he was appointed president of the Junta of Commerce and Money, and promoted the es
2 CONC tablishment of chartered corporations for the improvement of regional trade and manufacture.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The new king Ferdinand VI appointed him secretary of State (foreign minister) that same year, and carried out a neutrality policy. In 1750, he signed the agreement between Spain and Portugal that finished the disputes over the b
2 CONC orders of Río de la Plata and Brazil; Colonia del Sacramento returned to Spain in exchange of some Paraguayan territories.
2 CONT * He reformed the royal mail and in 1752 founded definitively the Real Academia de Bellas Artes de San Fernando.
2 CONT ====Bibliography====
2 CONT
2 CONT     Molina, Juan: José de Carvajal: un ministro para el reformismo borbónico, Cáceres: Institución Cultural El Brocense, 1999
2 CONT     Delgado, José Miguel: El proyecto político de Carvajal: pensamiento y reforma en tiempos de Fernando VI, Madrid: CSIC, 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT     ^ Real Academia de Bellas Artes de San Fernando
