1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Maria Antonia Montoya''', born 1682 in New Mexico; died August 22, 1750 in Santa Fe, New Mexico. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Antonio Montoya and Maria Hurtado. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She appeared on the Don Diego de Vargas census 22 Dec 1692 to 2 Jan 1693 in El Paso del Norte, Nuevo Méjico. Nueva España.
2 CONT
2 CONT First household, three sons, five daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT First, I (Vargas) visited the household of captain Antonio Montoya, alcalde ordinario de segundo voto, married to María Hurtado, with eight children, three boys and five girls; Juan, eighteen, Andrés, fourteen, and Antonio, nursin
2 CONC g; Juana, thirteen; María, eleven; Antonia, seven; Nicolasa, five; Tomasa, three.
2 CONT
2 CONT To The Royal Crown Restored, pg. 37
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/110326636/person/120146214273 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 12 2017, 15:05:42 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT From Overtons/Dameys and Related Families
2 CONT
2 CONT https://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=overtons&id=I17859
2 CONT
2 CONT Miguel was more often referred to as Miguel de Ia Vega y Coca. No baptismal records have been located for don Miguel. According to Fray Angelico Chavez, " Miguel came to New Mexico with the colonists of 1693. He was sixteen years 
2 CONC old, fair-complexed, with an quiline face and small eyes."
2 CONT
2 CONT Esquibel and Colligan write, " At about the age of sixteen, in the summer of 1693, Miguel responded to the recruitment efforts made by Fray Franciso Farfan and Capitain Cristobal de Velasco. . . before Miguel could completely qual
2 CONC ify it was necessary that he be married. . . he was able to arrange a marriage. '''On 19 July 1693, Miguel de la Vega and Maria Manuela de Medina were married''' by Farfan with Captain Cristóbal de Velasco and fray Agustin de Coca
2 CONC  as official witnessses. In late August, this marriage was recorded in the marriage book of espanoles for the Catedral de Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed on the muster roll of colonists made sometime between July 8-July 15, 1693 were: Miguel; his wife, Manuela de Medina 17, and Miguel's mother-in-law, Josefa de Cabrera with Pedro Valentin, Josefa's nineteen year-old nephew. 
2 CONC Josefa de Cabrera was noted as the " suegra" of Miguel de la Vega y Coca. Miguel de Ia Vega y Coca, was the son of Cristobal de Ia Vega, native of Mexico, age16, able-bodied, aquiline face, white, with small eyes; Manuela de Medin
2 CONC a, his wife, daughter of Alonso, native of Mexico, age 17, able-bodied, swarthy coloring, large eyes, small and somewhat fat nose; Josefa de Cabrera, age 30 and the widow of Alonso de Medina. Josesefa was listed as mother of the s
2 CONC aid Manuela, native of Mexico, with an aquiline face, large eyes, and a sharp nose.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Vega y Coca family were members of the Catedral de Mexico sagrario. The sagrario is a church annexed to a cathedral in which sacraments are administered and where the parishioners are registered by census. A description of thi
2 CONC s family group, without Pedro Valentin, is found in the detailed Mexico City roll of September 1-3,1693. On September I, 1693 they received their travel allowance of 300 pesos. All members of this family arrived and settled in San
2 CONC ta Fe. (Esquibel:49 &393)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Miguel was a widower by 1697'''. He was residing in the Santa Cruz area. Two years later '''he petitioned to marry dona Maria Montoya, a native of New Mexico and a daughter of Antonio Montoya and Maria Hurtado'''. There were no
2 CONC  impediments and this couple was soon married. They were residents of the Santa Fe area where Miguel acquired land and where he and Maria Montoya raised eight daughters. '''Captain Miguel de la Vega y Coca had one known son, Anton
2 CONC io Jose Laso de la Vega y Vic(sic) Vique. It is uncertain whether his mother was Maria Manuela Medina, dona Maria Montoya, or possibly a mistress.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 9, 1714, Captain Sebastian de Vargas, a resident of Santa Fe, gave his compadre Miguel de Ia Vega one share of the six shares he owned of the mine named Nuestra Señora de los Reyes Linares located in the San Lázaro mounta
2 CONC ins at a place called El Tuerto. This gift was given so that Miguel might be able to work the mine and have the profits from whatever ores were extracted. There is no documentation to inform us if he was successful in his mining o
2 CONC perations and documents regarding the settlement of his estate do not mention the mine. It is probable that Miguel sold or gave his share to someone else since he made his living from his ranching activities and civil positions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miguel served as a soldier of the Santa Fe militia, participating in several campaigns against hostile groups of Apaches and Utes. The earliest recorded account of his military service is from the year 1715 when he held the rank o
2 CONC f Alferez (Ensign), during a campaign against the Apaches. When a council was held in 1719 prior to Governor don Antonio de Valverde y Cosio?s campaign against the Utes and Comanches, Captain Miguel de la Vega y Coca stated he had
2 CONC  been a militia man for twenty-five years, and urged that war be made on the Utes. In 1724, "don" Miguel de la Vega y Coca gave testimony concerning the ill-fated Villasur expedition, declaring he was a resident of Santa Fe and ha
2 CONC d lived in New Mexico for thirty years, giving his age as fifty-four and signing his name as Miguel Joseph de Ia Vega y Coca.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miguel and doña Maria Montoya were still residing in the Santa Fe area in 1727 when they sold some land to Josefa López. After this time, and until around 1731, the Vega y Coca family lived in the Taos area where Miguel held sever
2 CONC al consecutive terms as Alcalde Mayor of Taos and Picuris. They returned to La Ciénega to settle at their ranch, about twelve miles from Santa Fe.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1750 at about the age of seventy-three, don Miguel de Vega y Coca was still living, as was his wife, dona Maria Montoya. They appeared in the 1750 census of Alamo, in the jurisdiction of Santa Fe, with seven other individuals i
2 CONC n their household: "Don Miguel Coca; his wife; Juan Esteban; Xptoval; Juan Antonio, Juan (sic) Barbara; 2 children." There was no information to confirm that any the individuals listed in don Miguel household were actually his chi
2 CONC dren. '''By the latter part of 1750, he was a widower again.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Miguel came to New Mexico as a young man with the hopes of securing his fortune on the frontier. He was successful in his endeavors. He acquired property; held important civil and military positions. Don Miguel de Vega y Coca 
2 CONC earned the respect as a man of honor and nobility (Esquibel:393 &394).
2 CONT
2 CONT Diego de Coca was baptized at Pueblo de la Laguna, New Mexico, on August 8, 1732. He was identified as a "hijo de Padres Gentiles." His padrino was Miguel José de la Vega y Coca. The priest who baptized Diego de Coca was fray Caye
2 CONC tano de Otero in the place of the regular priest, Fray Francisco Lorchundi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Researcher: José Antonio Esquibel, Source: AASF Roll 5, San José de Laguna Church, Baptismal Records 1720-1776.
