1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elena Gallegos, born about 1680, was the daughter of Antonio Gallegos and Catarina Baca of Bernalillo, New Mexico. Her parents had escaped New Mexico at the time of the Pueblo Revolt of 1680, and Elena and her two 
2 CONC brothers returned to New Mexico around 1693 at the time of the De Vargas Reconquest. At the age of 19, she married Santiago Gurulé. After Santiago's death in 1711, she conducted her own business affairs. She was a stock raiser, an
2 CONC d was the first woman to record her own brand in 1712. About 1716, she acquired the Jesus Maria Grant, sold to her by Diego Montoya’s son. Much of what is now North Albuquerque is built on the 35,084 acre Elena Gallegos land grant
2 CONC . At the foothills of the Sandia Mountains is a sign to mark the entrance to the Elena Gallegos Park.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Elena Gallegos, born about 1680, was the daughter of Antonio Gallegos and Catarina Baca of Bernalillo, New Mexico. Her parents had escaped New Mexico at the time of the Pueblo Revolt of 1680, and Elena and her two brothers returne
2 CONC d to New Mexico around 1693 at the time of the De Vargas Reconquest. At the age of 19, she married Santiago Gurulé (aka Jacques Grolet). After Santiago's death in 1711, she conducted her own business affairs. She was a stock raise
2 CONC r, and was the first woman to record her own brand in 1712. About 1716, she acquired the Jesus Maria Grant, sold to her by Diego Montoya’s son. Much of what is now North Albuquerque is built on the 35,084 acre Elena Gallegos land 
2 CONC grant. At the foothills of the Sandia Mountains is a sign to mark the entrance to the Elena Gallegos Park.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  http://www.gurulefamily.org
2 CONT
2 CONT Translated will of Elena Gallegos:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.gurulefamily.org/ancestry/elena.shtml
2 CONT
2 CONT Spanish  version of Elena's will:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://pages.prodigy.net/bluemountain1/beyondorigins.htm.
2 CONT
2 CONT The New Mexico History State Records Center and Archive provides a detailed description of the Elena Gallegos Land Grant here: https://newmexicohistory.org/2015/07/22/the-elena-gallegos-grant/. The write up also provides the locat
2 CONC ions of many source documents. 
2 CONT
